/*  FIT A BINARY PAIR TO MEASURED DATA, THEN TEST IT ON DATA IT NEVER SAW.

    `fitParameters` regresses the four NRTL coefficients of the ethanol-water
    pair on EIGHT measured bubble temperatures at 101.325 kPa, then predicts
    THREE further measured points that were frozen out of the fit before
    anything was run (x1 = 0.20, 0.50, 0.80).  The band the held-out error
    must meet (0.1 % of T) was declared before the fit and is not adjusted.

    What to read in the output, in order:
      * FIT QUALITY       -- what the model does on the points it saw;
      * HELD-OUT EVIDENCE -- what it does on the three it did not: the
                             average absolute deviation and the verdict;
      * identifiability   -- the four numbers are NOT individually determined
                             by one isobar (the run says so), yet the pair
                             predicts well: validated is not the same as
                             determined.

    The run writes `ethanol-water.proposal.dat`, the fitted pair with its
    evidence, its verdict and that warning in its own header, plus the fit
    log and the parity table.  The full design is in system/propsDict. */
tier          tutorial;
application   choupoProps;
description     "A binary NRTL pair regressed on eight MEASURED bubble temperatures and scored on three it never saw -- the first held-out validation in the corpus against real evidence";
verbosity     3;
